{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01262 - MONDO_15667",
  "verbalization": "Decitabine, a DNA methyltransferase inhibitor, is implicated in the treatment of Therapy-Related Acute Myeloid Leukemia (t-AML) and Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS), as evidenced by its indication in these conditions. The evidence suggests that Decitabine may exert its therapeutic effect through mechanisms that target the epigenetic dysregulation characteristic of these diseases. The path evidence links Decitabine to Ivosidenib and Gilteritinib, both of which are inhibitors of the PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway, and these drugs are indicated for Unclassified Acute Myeloid Leukemia. This suggests a potential pathway where Decitabine may influence the same or related molecular processes in AML, possibly through epigenetic modulation. The presence of overlapping indications and the shared mode of action between Decitabine and its associated drugs indicate a plausible mechanistic link, though the exact mechanism of Decitabine's effect on Unclassified AML remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a potential role for Decitabine in the treatment of this leukemia subtype, but further experimental validation is required to confirm the direct relationship.",
